---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Scholarship"
	description="Gavin Keulks — chapters and essays on the Amises, postmodernism and contemporary fiction, visual culture, and Aldous Huxley, plus the Martin Amis Web."
>
	<div class="wrap">
		<header class="page-head">
			<h1>Scholarship</h1>
			<p class="page-intro">
				Work for both general and specialized audiences, organized by theme.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="archive">
			<h2>The Martin Amis Web</h2>
			<p>
				I have overseen and administered{' '}
				<a href="https://martinamisweb.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Martin&nbsp;Amis Web</a> since
				2006 &mdash; the authoritative digital archive for the author. The British
				Library designated it a site of scholarly significance in 2012 and
				archives it every six months as part of their ongoing UK Web Archive
				project. I rebuilt the site in 2006 and completely overhauled and
				modernized it in 2026.
			</p>
			<p>
				I designed and maintained three other sites in the same period: the WOU
				Honors Program (2008&ndash;2026), Faculty Senate (2010&ndash;2012), and
				English Department (2007&ndash;2009) webpages.
			</p>
			<p>
				Administering a digital archive for a famous figure has been not only a
				responsibility but also a subject of my research, spanning numerous
				conference papers and presentations.
			</p>
			<ul class="record-list">
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2017</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Authorship in the Age of Digital Archiving.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Technology, Knowledge, and Society International Conference &middot; Toronto,&nbsp;Canada</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2012</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Shadowy Margins and Sinkholes: Authorship, Online Archives, and IP&nbsp;Law.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; Boston,&nbsp;MA</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2011</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;The Rhetoric of Road-Rage: Authorial Websites and Legal Hit-and-Runs.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; San&nbsp;Antonio,&nbsp;TX</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2008</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Intellectual Properties: Growth Boundaries in Digital Scholarship and Copyright Law.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Southwest/Texas Popular Culture Association &middot; Albuquerque,&nbsp;NM</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2007</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Digital Humanizing: Administering Author/Scholar Relationships.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Society for Digital Humanities &middot; Saskatoon,&nbsp;Canada</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2007</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Administering Author Websites: Evolving Challenges in Scholarship, Copyright, and Discussion.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; Boston,&nbsp;MA</span>
					</span>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="work">
			<h2>Kingsley and Martin Amis</h2>
			<ul class="cite-list">
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Staging Amis: Autobiography and Autofiction.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>The Cambridge&nbsp;Companion to Martin&nbsp;Amis</em>. Cambridge
					University Press, 2026. 211&ndash;27. Also the Introduction, 1&ndash;10.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;&lsquo;No less than we deserve&rsquo;: the Traditional Roots
						of the Amises&rsquo; Contemporary Satires.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Critical Engagements</em> 3.2 (satire issue, Winter 2010): 35&ndash;51.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;W(h)ither Postmodernism: Late Amis.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Martin Amis: Postmodernism and Beyond</em>. Palgrave Macmillan,
					2006. 158&ndash;79. Also the Introduction, 1&ndash;8.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="work">
			<h2>Postmodernism and Contemporary Fiction</h2>
			<ul class="cite-list">
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;&lsquo;Preferring the composite darkness&rsquo;: Trauma Theory
