					>{' '}
					<em>The Mourning After: Attending the Wake of Postmodernism</em>. Eds.
					Neil Brooks and Josh Toth. Rodopi, 2007. 143&ndash;68.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Winterson&rsquo;s Recent Work: Navigating Realism and
						Postmodernism.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Jeanette Winterson: A Contemporary Critical Guide</em>. Ed. Sonya
					Andermahr. Continuum, 2007. 146&ndash;62.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="work">
			<h2>Visual Culture and Advertising</h2>
			<ul class="cite-list">
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Visualizing the Virtual: Alejandro
						G.&nbsp;I&ntilde;&aacute;rritu&rsquo;s{' '}
						<em>CARNE y ARENA</em>, A Case Study.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Advertising and Society</em>. Cognella. Accepted for publication.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;The Artful Marketing of Irish Culture: Dublin&rsquo;s Icon
						Walk.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Advertising and Society</em>. Cognella. Accepted for publication.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;YOLO&rsquo;ing your Book Launch, or (ahem) Multi-channel
						Cross-promotion for the Digital Age.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					Invited blogpost. <em>Fifteen Eighty Four</em>, Academic Perspectives
					from Cambridge University&nbsp;Press. 21&nbsp;July&nbsp;2026.{' '}
					<a
						href="https://cambridgeblog.org/2026/07/yoloing-your-book-launch-or-ahemmulti-channel-cross-promotionfor-the-digital-age/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>cambridgeblog.org</a
					>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="work">
			<h2>Huxley and Earlier Work</h2>
			<ul class="cite-list">
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Remembrance of Mentorship Past &mdash; or, Marlow at the
						Shuffleboard.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Aldous Huxley: Modern Satirical Novelist of Ideas</em>. Eds. Peter
					Firchow and Bernfried Nugel. Human Potentialities, vol.&nbsp;8. LIT
					Verlag, 2006. xi&ndash;xvi.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Aldous Huxley: A Centenary Bibliography
						(1978&ndash;1995).&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Journal of Modern Literature</em> 20.2 (Winter 1996): 223&ndash;38.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Grounds for Debate: The Nature of R.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;Thomas&rsquo;s
						Early Poetry and Prose.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>Proceedings of the First Annual Conference on Welsh Studies</em>.
					NAASWCH, 1996. 95&ndash;102.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="work">
			<h2>Reference</h2>
			<ul class="cite-list">
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>Nine entries for the{' '}
						<em>Oxford Encyclopedia of British Literature</em>.</span
					>{' '}
					Eds. David Scott Kastan et&nbsp;al. Oxford University Press, 2006. On
					Elizabeth Bowen, Arthur Conan Doyle, Graham Greene, Aldous Huxley, Kazuo
					Ishiguro, David Lodge, Edna O&rsquo;Brien, Salman Rushdie, and Jeanette
					Winterson.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title"
						>&ldquo;Kingsley Amis&rdquo; and &ldquo;Martin Amis.&rdquo;</span
					>{' '}
					<em>The Blackwell Encyclopedia of Twentieth-Century Fiction</em>. Eds.
					Brian Shaffer et&nbsp;al. Blackwell, 2011.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Kingsley Amis.&rdquo;</span>{' '}
					<em>The Literary Encyclopedia</em>. Eds. Robert Clark, Emory Elliott, and
